## 1. What is it?

"DOM fully loaded" detection is how you know it's safe to run code that touches the page's elements — and it actually splits into two distinct, commonly-confused signals: `DOMContentLoaded` (the HTML has been fully parsed into the DOM tree) and `load` (everything — images, stylesheets, iframes, subresources — has finished loading too). There's also `document.readyState`, a synchronous property for checking the current state at any point, including after the events themselves have already fired.

## 2. Why introduced?

Early scripts ran inline, in document order, which meant a `<script>` tag could execute before elements later in the HTML even existed — a constant source of "cannot read property of null" bugs when a script tried to grab an element by ID too early. Browsers needed explicit lifecycle events so code could declare "run this once the DOM (or the whole page) is actually ready," instead of developers guessing based on script placement or wrapping everything in fragile `setTimeout` hacks.

## 3. How does it work?

`document.readyState` moves through three values over a page load: `"loading"` (still parsing HTML), `"interactive"` (parsing finished, DOM is fully built and accessible, but subresources like images/stylesheets/iframes may still be loading), and `"complete"` (everything has finished loading, including subresources).

`DOMContentLoaded` fires on `document` the moment `readyState` transitions to `"interactive"` — as soon as the DOM tree is parsed and safe to query/manipulate, without waiting for images or stylesheets. It does wait for synchronous, non-deferred `<script>` tags to finish executing (since the parser blocks on those), but not for async scripts or subresources.

`load` fires on `window` the moment `readyState` transitions to `"complete"` — every image, stylesheet, and iframe has finished loading. This is strictly later than (or simultaneous with) `DOMContentLoaded`, never earlier.

Because both events are one-shot and fire once per page load, attaching a listener *after* the event has already happened means you'll never see it fire — which is exactly what `readyState` solves: check it synchronously first, and only attach a listener if the target state hasn't been reached yet.

```javascript
function onDomReady(callback) {
  if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', callback);
  } else {
    // readyState is already 'interactive' or 'complete' — DOMContentLoaded already fired
    callback();
  }
}
```

Modern HTML also offers `<script defer>`, which delays script execution until parsing completes (effectively running right before `DOMContentLoaded`) without blocking the parser, and `<script type="module">`, which defers by default for the same reason — both largely replace the "put your script tag at the bottom of `<body>`" convention.

## 4. Example

```html
<script>
  console.log('readyState at top of <head>:', document.readyState); // "loading"

  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    console.log('DOM parsed, safe to query elements:', document.readyState); // "interactive"
    console.log(document.getElementById('main')); // found — DOM exists
  });

  window.addEventListener('load', () => {
    console.log('Everything loaded, including images:', document.readyState); // "complete"
  });
</script>

<body>
  <div id="main">
    <img src="large-photo.jpg">
  </div>
</body>
```

In an Angular app specifically, you rarely reach for these directly — Angular's own bootstrap (`bootstrapApplication`/`platformBrowserDynamic().bootstrapModule()`) already runs after the DOM is parseable, and finer-grained "has the view actually rendered" hooks are handled by Angular's own lifecycle (`ngAfterViewInit`) or, in modern Angular, `afterNextRender()`/`afterRender()` for code that specifically needs the browser DOM (e.g., measuring an element, or third-party libraries that read layout).

```typescript
import { Component, afterNextRender, ElementRef, viewChild } from '@angular/core';

@Component({ selector: 'app-chart', template: `<div #chartHost></div>` })
export class ChartComponent {
  chartHost = viewChild.required<ElementRef<HTMLDivElement>>('chartHost');

  constructor() {
    afterNextRender(() => {
      // Guaranteed the view's DOM nodes exist and are attached — safe to measure/mount a chart library here.
      console.log(this.chartHost().nativeElement.getBoundingClientRect());
    });
  }
}
```

## 5. Example Explanation – Execution Flow

At the top of the inline `<script>` in `<head>`, the parser hasn't reached `<body>` yet, so `readyState` reads `"loading"`. The `DOMContentLoaded` listener is registered before that event can fire, so it reliably runs once parsing completes — at that point `getElementById('main')` succeeds because the DOM tree is fully built, even though `large-photo.jpg` may still be mid-download. The `load` listener fires later still, once the image finishes downloading and `readyState` reaches `"complete"`. In the Angular example, `afterNextRender()` schedules its callback for after Angular has rendered the component's view into real DOM nodes and attached them — guaranteeing `chartHost().nativeElement` is a real, attached element by the time the callback runs, which is the Angular-idiomatic equivalent of "wait for the relevant DOM to exist" scoped to a single component rather than the whole document.

## 6. When does this show up?

Any vanilla-JS or third-party-script integration where you must query or mutate the DOM but can't guarantee script execution order — widget embeds, analytics snippets, non-Angular libraries mounted into a page. Within Angular apps it shows up specifically when integrating DOM-measuring or DOM-mutating third-party libraries (charting, maps, rich text editors) that need real, attached DOM nodes and can't run inside Angular's virtual/pre-render lifecycle — hence reaching for `afterNextRender()` instead of, say, `ngOnInit()` (which can run before view DOM is attached in some scenarios) or the vanilla `DOMContentLoaded`/`load` events (which fire once for the whole document, not per-component).

## 7. Advantages/Disadvantages

`DOMContentLoaded` is fast — code runs as soon as the DOM is safe to touch, without waiting on slow images. The tradeoff is that anything depending on image dimensions, iframe content, or other subresources isn't ready yet, so using it for layout-dependent code can misfire. `load` guarantees everything is ready but is correspondingly slower and blocks meaningful work behind potentially large downloads. Checking `readyState` directly is the safest general pattern since it works regardless of whether the event already fired, but requires remembering to branch on it — a plain `addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', cb)` with no `readyState` check silently does nothing if the script runs after the event already happened (e.g., a dynamically injected `<script>` tag added late).

## 8. Common Interview Traps

Confusing `DOMContentLoaded` (DOM parsed) with `load` (everything, including images, finished) — a very common mix-up. Forgetting that a listener attached after the event already fired simply never runs, and not knowing `readyState` is the fix. Assuming `defer`/`type="module"` scripts run *during* parsing — they're fetched during parsing but execute after parsing completes, in document order, just before `DOMContentLoaded`. In Angular specifically: assuming `ngOnInit()` guarantees the view's DOM is rendered and attached — it doesn't always (it runs after inputs are set, before/around view initialization depending on the case) — `ngAfterViewInit()` or `afterNextRender()` are the correct hooks for DOM-dependent work.

## 9. Interview Follow-up Questions

What's the execution-order difference between a `defer` script and an `async` script relative to `DOMContentLoaded`?

Why might `window.onload` fire much later than expected on a page with many images?

In Angular, when would you reach for `afterNextRender()` vs `afterRender()` vs `ngAfterViewInit()`?

How would you detect "DOM ready" for content injected dynamically via `innerHTML` or a framework, where no native browser event fires at all?

## 10. Quick Answer (say this first)

Use `DOMContentLoaded` when you need the DOM tree parsed and queryable but don't care about images/stylesheets, and `window.load` when you need everything — including subresources — fully loaded. Always guard with `document.readyState` first, since both events are one-shot and a listener attached after the fact will never fire. In Angular, the framework-idiomatic equivalent for "the DOM I care about exists" is `afterNextRender()`/`afterRender()`, scoped to a component rather than the whole document.

## 11. Interview-Ready Answer (full)

Detecting a fully-loaded DOM actually means picking the right signal for what "loaded" means to your code. `document.readyState` moves through `"loading"` → `"interactive"` → `"complete"`. `DOMContentLoaded` fires the moment `readyState` hits `"interactive"` — the HTML has been fully parsed into a DOM tree, so it's safe to query and manipulate elements, but images, stylesheets, and iframes may still be loading. `window`'s `load` event fires later, once `readyState` reaches `"complete"`, meaning every subresource has finished too. Because both are one-time events, the safe general pattern is to check `readyState` synchronously first — if it's already past `"loading"`, the DOM is ready and you can run immediately; otherwise, attach the listener and wait, since a listener registered after the event already fired will simply never be called. In Angular, you rarely touch these directly — Angular's bootstrap already runs post-parse, and for code that specifically needs real, attached DOM nodes (measuring an element, mounting a third-party chart or map library), the idiomatic hook is `afterNextRender()` (or `afterRender()` for recurring post-render work), which guarantees the component's view has been rendered into actual DOM, scoped to that component rather than the whole document.

## 12. Quick Revision

`readyState`: `"loading"` → `"interactive"` (DOM parsed, `DOMContentLoaded` fires here) → `"complete"` (all subresources loaded, `load` fires here). Both events are one-shot — check `readyState` before attaching a listener, since post-hoc listeners never fire. `defer`/`module` scripts execute right before `DOMContentLoaded`, in order, without blocking the parser. Angular equivalent: `afterNextRender()`/`afterRender()` for DOM-dependent work per component, not `ngOnInit()`.

## 13. Memory Trick

**"DOMContentLoaded = the skeleton's built. `load` = the skeleton's dressed, fed, and the photos have arrived. Check `readyState` first — you can't RSVP to a party that already happened."**
